  <data name="translation.help.battery.avgDoD" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Average Depth of Discharge per cycle — how deeply the pack is typically discharged before being recharged. Shallower cycles cause less wear.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.battery.capacity" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Estimated current usable energy capacity of the pack in kWh, derived from the SoH and the original rated capacity.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.battery.degradationRate" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Annualised rate of capacity loss based on observed SoH trend. Combines calendar fade (time at temperature/SoC) and cycle fade (kWh throughput).</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.battery.soh" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>State of Health — current usable capacity divided by the original rated capacity, expressed as a percentage. Higher is better; new packs start at 100%.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.battery.totalCycles" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Cumulative full-pack equivalent cycles. One cycle = one full discharge + one full charge worth of energy (partial cycles add up over time).</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.charging.avgPower" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Time-averaged charging power across the session. Always lower than peak when the session includes a taper or initial ramp.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.charging.chargeCurve" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Power vs SoC curve for the session. Tapering — the gradual drop in power as the battery approaches full — is inherent to lithium chemistry and is not a fault. Sudden drops below the curve indicate derating: the charger or battery is throttling power because of cell or ambient temperature limits.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.charging.kwhPerHour" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Average kWh added per hour across the session. Useful for estimating how long a future session will take at the same charger.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.charging.peakPower" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Maximum charging power observed during this session, in kW. DC fast-charging tapers above ~80% SoC; AC charging stays roughly flat.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.charging.socRange" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The starting and ending state-of-charge percentages for this session. Wider ranges generally mean longer sessions and more taper.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.cooldownMin.body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Minimum minutes between consecutive alerts from this rule, regardless of how often the underlying signal crosses the threshold.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.driveScore.body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>0–100 score derived from smoothness of acceleration, braking, and cornering combined with energy efficiency. Tunable in Settings → Driving.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.driveScore.iconLabel" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>More info about Drive Score</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.alertStudio.alertBehavior" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Pick 'Notify on event' for one-time confirmations like 'vehicle locked' or 'charging done'. Pick 'Re-alert until resolved' for ongoing safety concerns like 'vehicle unlocked' or 'door open'.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.alertStudio.cooldown" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Minimum minutes between repeat firings of this rule. Prevents the same condition from spamming your channels (e.g. battery at 19% triggering once per minute).</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.alertStudio.kind" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Signal threshold compares a single live telemetry value (e.g. BatteryLevel &lt; 20). Computed metric aggregates over a time window (e.g. cost &gt; $5 in last 24h).</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.alertStudio.operator" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Comparison applied to the signal value: =, !=, &lt;, &lt;=, &gt;, &gt;=, between, any change. Some operators only apply to numeric signals.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.alertStudio.severity" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Visual / routing priority — info, warn, or critical. Channels can subscribe to specific severities to keep critical alerts from being drowned out by chatty info ones.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.alertStudio.testMessage" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Message body sent when the rule fires. Use {0} placeholders to interpolate the current value into the notification.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.alertStudio.vehicleId" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Restricts the rule to a single vehicle. Leave blank to apply across every vehicle on the account.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.automations.conditionLogic" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>When multiple conditions are configured, ALL must pass (AND) or ANY must pass (OR) for the actions to run.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.automations.cronExpr" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Standard 5-field cron syntax (minute hour day-of-month month day-of-week). Use the simple mode above for the most common schedules.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.automations.debounce" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Minimum milliseconds between consecutive runs of this automation. Protects against duplicate triggers when a signal flickers across the threshold.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.automations.dwellMinutes" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>How many minutes the vehicle must stay inside the geofence before this dwell trigger fires.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.automations.geofence" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A named circular region defined in /locations. Automations can react when the vehicle enters or exits the region.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.automations.operator" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>How the trigger or condition value is compared (=, !=, &lt;, &gt;, between, etc.). Numeric operators are only available for numeric signals.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.automations.otherAutomation" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Make this automation depend on another automation's recent state — e.g. only run if 'Morning prep' has not run today.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.automations.signal" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The vehicle telemetry signal this condition reads. Booleans use true/false, 'state' uses keywords like online/asleep, all others compare numeric values.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.automations.timeWindow" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Time-of-day range (e.g. 22:00–06:00) and timezone. The condition only passes during that window in the chosen timezone.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.automations.timezone" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>IANA time zone used to interpret the start/end window. Defaults to your browser zone if left blank.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.automations.triggerType" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>What kicks the automation off — a time schedule, a signal threshold crossing, a geofence enter/exit, or another automation completing.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.channels.bodyTemplate" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Optional JSON template for the request body. Use {0}, {1}, {2} placeholders. If omitted, TeslaSync sends its default JSON envelope.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.channels.botToken" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Telegram BotFather token in the format `&lt;id&gt;:&lt;secret&gt;`. Create a bot via @BotFather on Telegram, then start a chat with it before TeslaSync can deliver.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.channels.chatId" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Numeric Telegram chat ID. For a private bot chat this is your user ID; for a group it's negative (e.g. -1001234567890).</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.channels.configSection" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Provider-specific credentials and routing details. Required fields vary by channel type. All secrets are encrypted at rest.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.channels.headersJson" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Extra HTTP headers as a JSON object — e.g. {"Authorization": "Bearer XYZ"}. Useful for receivers that require API keys outside the URL.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.channels.hmacSecret" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Optional shared secret. When set, TeslaSync signs every request with HMAC-SHA256 in the X-TeslaSync-Signature header so receivers can verify authenticity.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.channels.method" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>HTTP method TeslaSync uses for outbound delivery. POST is universal; some receivers require PUT or PATCH.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.channels.nameLabel" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Friendly identifier shown in the channel list and on alert delivery logs. Has no functional impact — pick anything memorable.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.channels.ntfyServer" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Base URL of the ntfy server (e.g. https://ntfy.sh for the public server, or your self-hosted instance).</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.channels.ntfyTopic" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Topic name on the ntfy server. Anyone with the topic name can subscribe — pick something hard to guess for sensitive alerts.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.channels.smtpHost" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Mail server hostname (e.g. smtp.gmail.com). Most providers use port 587 with STARTTLS; check your provider's docs for the right host + port pair.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.channels.smtpPassword" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>SMTP password or app-specific password. Stored encrypted at rest. Many providers (Gmail, Microsoft 365) require an app password rather than your account password.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.channels.smtpPort" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>SMTP server port. 587 for STARTTLS (recommended), 465 for implicit TLS, 25 for plain (insecure — only on local relays).</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.channels.testHint" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Use the "Send Test" button after saving to verify your configuration. Tests bypass severity filters but otherwise match real delivery.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.channels.webhookUrl" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Full HTTPS URL TeslaSync POSTs the JSON body to. Discord and Slack provide their own webhook endpoints; for custom services use the generic Webhook channel.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.settings.appearanceDensity" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Controls row height, padding, and font sizes across tables and dashboards. Compact fits more on screen; comfortable is easier to read on large displays.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.settings.chartPalette" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Colour scheme used for chart series. Vivid is the default high-contrast palette; muted reduces visual noise; print is grayscale-friendly for exports.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.settings.electricityCost" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Cost per kWh used to compute charging spend across drives, charging sessions, and TCO analytics. Currency follows the Currency setting above.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.settings.gasPriceAutoPoll" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>When enabled, TeslaSync automatically fetches U.S. average gasoline prices from the EIA. Used to compute fuel-savings vs. driving an EV.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.settings.gasPricePollInterval" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>How often the gas-price worker polls the EIA. Daily catches price spikes; weekly is plenty for most users and is gentler on the upstream feed.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.settings.language" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Display language for UI strings. TeslaSync ships English; community translations may be incomplete for newer features.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.settings.notificationChannels" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Where alert messages are delivered. Add channels (Discord, Slack, email, webhook, ntfy, Pushover, Telegram) before alerts can fire.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.settings.region" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Tesla account region (NA, EU, CN). Determines which Fleet API endpoint TeslaSync calls. Refresh after changing your Tesla account country.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.settings.timeFormat" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>12-hour (1:30 PM) vs 24-hour (13:30) display across timestamps. Affects all surfaces — drives, charging, alerts, telemetry charts.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.settings.uiTheme" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Light / dark / system theme. System follows your OS preference and switches automatically when your OS toggles between modes.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fields.settings.weekStart" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>First day of the week in calendar pickers and weekly aggregations. ISO 8601 default is Monday; many U.S. users prefer Sunday.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fleetTelemetryHealthy.body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Last MQTT message received within the last 5 minutes. Stale means no recent message; check the fleet-telemetry deployment.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fsm.liveState" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The current state the FSM resolved to from the most recent telemetry. The FSM stays in a terminal state until external evidence (telemetry or poll) triggers an explicit transition out.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.fsm.type" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Finite-state machine. Tracks vehicle high-level state (driving, charging, parked, online, asleep, offline) and the transitions between them. Sub-FSMs cover drive, charge, command, and notification lifecycles.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.intro" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Get started with TeslaSync. The links below cover the most common questions; for anything else, ask the in-app assistant or open the documentation.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.lifetime.avgEfficiency" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Average energy used per unit distance across the whole driving history (Wh/km). Lower is better — temperature, speed, and terrain are the main drivers.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.regenEfficiency.body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Energy recovered through regenerative braking divided by total energy used during driving. Higher is better — Tesla cars typically reach 15–30% recovery in mixed driving.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.regenEfficiency.iconLabel" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>More info about regen metrics</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.replay.battery" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>State-of-charge percentage at this point. Drops indicate energy use; rises indicate regen or DC-fast-charging during a drive.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.replay.power" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Instantaneous battery power at this point on the trip. Negative values indicate regenerative braking (energy flowing back into the pack); positive values indicate motor draw.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.replay.range" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Estimated rated range remaining at this position based on EPA rated efficiency. Differs from real-world range, which depends on speed, terrain, climate, and load.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.signal.deltaCol" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Numeric difference (and percent change) between Window A and Window B for this signal. 'changed' is shown for non-numeric values that differ.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.signal.diff" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Server-side comparison between two snapshots. Unchanged signals are omitted from the result to reduce noise.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.signal.layers" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>TeslaSync exposes three live-state layers: L1 — in-process signal store on the API pod that ingested the telemetry (lowest latency, scope-limited to that pod). L2 — Redis-backed shared cache of latest values, visible to every pod, used for cross-pod live reads and SSE fanout. log — TimescaleDB signal_log history, authoritative for analytics, charts, and point-in-time reconstruction.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.signal.live" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Live mode streams real-time signal values via Server-Sent Events (SSE). The chart maintains a rolling 5-minute window throttled to 2 Hz updates.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.signal.snapshot" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A snapshot is a point-in-time view of every signal value at a single timestamp. The diff falls back to signal_log within the last 30 days when the live layer doesn't have it.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.signal.sourceLayer" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The layer that supplied this value: L1 (in-process), L2 (Redis), LOG (TimescaleDB history), or STALE (older than 2 minutes).</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.signal.stale" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A signal is marked stale when its last-seen value in Redis is older than 2 minutes — treat with caution; the vehicle may be offline or the pipeline may be backed up.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.sleepEfficiency.body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Share of parked time the car spent in true low-power sleep (vs. idle/online). Higher is better — more sleep means less vampire drain and lower battery wear.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.sleepEfficiency.sentryCost" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Estimated monthly electricity cost of Sentry-related drain, using your configured per-kWh rate.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.sleepEfficiency.sentryDrain" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Battery loss per hour while Sentry Mode is active. Sentry keeps cameras and computers on, which adds noticeable drain.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.sleepEfficiency.timeToSleep" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Average minutes from when the car parks to when it enters low-power sleep.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Help</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.tooltip.iconLabel" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>More info</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.vampireDrain.avgRate" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Mean battery loss per hour while parked and not charging across the selected period.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.vampireDrain.body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Idle energy lost while the car is parked and not charging. We compute it as the % of battery used per hour while the vehicle reports gear=Park and is not in motion.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.vampireDrain.score" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>0–100 health score derived from your drain rate vs. fleet expectations. Higher is better.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.vampireDrain.totalLoss" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Estimated kWh lost to vampire drain across all parked sessions in the selected period.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="translation.help.vampireDrain.worstSession" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Single parked session with the largest % battery drop.</value>
  </data>
